abstract:
We study the gradient superconvergence of bilinear finite volume element (FVE) solving the elliptic problems. First, a superclose weak estimate is established for the bilinear form of the FVE method. Then, we prove that the gradient approximation of the FVE solution has the superconvergence property: $\max_{P\in S}|(
abla u-\overline{
abla}u_h)(P)|=O(h^2)|\ln h|$, where $\overline{
abla}u_h(P)$ denotes the average gradient on elements containing point $P$ and $S$ is the set of optimal stress points composed of the mesh points, the midpoints of edges and elements.

This work deals with existence of solutions for the class of quasilinear elliptic problems with cylindrical singularities and multiple critical nonlinearities that can be written in the form  egin{align*} -\operatorname{div}\left[ rac{|
abla u|^{p-2}}{|y|^{ap}}
abla uight] -\mu\, rac{u^{p-1}}{|y|^{p(a+1)}} =  rac{u^{p^*(a,b)-1}}{|y|^{bp^*(a,b)}} +  rac{u^{p^*(a,c)-1}}{|y|^{cp^*(a,c)}}, \qquad (x,y) \in \mathbb{R}^{N-k}	imes\mathbb{R}^k. \end{align*} The existence of a positive, weak solution $u \in \mathcal{D}_a^{1,p}(\mathbb{R}^N ackslash\{|y|=0\})$ is proved with the help of the mountain pass theorem. We also prove a regularity result, that is, using Moser's iteration scheme we show that $u \in L_{\operatorname{loc}}^{\infty}(\Omega)$ for domains $\Omega \subset \mathbb{R}^{N-k}	imes\mathbb{R}^{k} ackslash \{ |y|=0 \}$ not necessarily bounded. Finally we show that if $ u \in \mathcal{D}_a^{1,p}(\mathbb{R}^N ackslash\{|y|=0\}) $ is a weak solution to the related problem  egin{align*} -\operatorname{div}\left[ rac{|
abla u|^{p-2}}{|y|^{ap}}
abla uight] -\mu\, rac{|u|^{p-2}u}{|y|^{p(a+1)}} =  rac{|u|^{q-2}u}{|y|^{bp^*(a,b)}} +  rac{|u|^{p^*(a,c)-2}u}{|y|^{cp^*(a,c)}}, \qquad (x,y) \in \mathbb{R}^{N-k}	imes\mathbb{R}^k, \end{align*} then $ u \equiv 0 $ when either $ 1 < q < p^*(a,b) $, or $ q > p^*(a,b) $ and $u \in L_{bp^*(a,b)/q, \operatorname{loc}}^{q} (\mathbb{R}^N ackslash\{|y|=0\}) \cap L_{\mathrm{loc}}^{\infty}(\mathbb{R}^{N-k}	imes \mathbb{R}^{k}  ackslash \{ |y| = 0\})$. This nonexistence of nontrivial solution is proved by using a Pohozaev-type identity.
